 # Theme: Software delivery planning
 
 ## Intent
-This domain turns a business intent into work that agents can build and that a check from the outside can prove. It serves the product owner, who names the work and approves its design, and the plays that build from that design. Its job comes before the build: it shapes one slice that a person can test end to end, it plans that slice as work items in the tracker, and it writes the eval that says if the intent is met. It does not build, check or release the work, and it never gives a builder the eval: the build is given the intent, and a check flags each build session that read the evals folder and records the flag in that build run's evidence.
+This domain turns a business intent into working code that a check from the outside can prove. It serves the product owner, who names the work, the technical lead, who designs it, and the developer, who builds it. It specifies the product, sets the project's design, and builds the work test first. It writes the eval that says if the work is met before the work is built, and it never gives a builder the eval or the tests: a check flags each build session that read them. It then tests the built work from outside the builder, by its evals and against the levels, surfaces, designs and standards set before the build. Once the product is in use, it takes in how the product is used, what people say about it and what its competitors do, works out what consumers want, and turns that into new features or changes to the model, as recommendations to the product owner or on their own where the product owner allows it. It also gets Garura itself into a project, sets the project up for it, keeps it current, and serves whichever coding tool the team works in. It also lands every change the same way, with an outside review and a person's yes, and gives people one tool to review what agents made. It also orchestrates the work: it chooses and orders what runs next, runs plays as loops, tracks the work in the team's tracker, records each decision on how the project is built as an ADR, and gives every gate a way past. It also keeps the foundation every part stands on: the written standards and their checks, the builders a team uses for its own plays and loops, where everything lives, the settings, three tiers of memory, and the model each part runs on. It also defines what a product is made of, and keeps each product's model as a living map: added to, changed and pruned as work is done, and the source of what to try next. It does not release the work or run the product.
 
