+# BI-10 close: the proposal shown to the product owner (draft, not applied)
+
+2026-11-11. BI-10 (Standards, structure, settings and memory) has its six capabilities in the model: Standards, Build your own harness, Structure, Settings, Memory, and Intelligent model routing. This is the close, as for BI-4 and BI-6 to BI-9. Nothing is applied before the product owner's yes.
+
+## 1. BI-10's page (`.garura/product/product-os/intents/bi-10.md`): kept as it is
+Each capability is already named on the page: "Everything is built on the ICE model with written standards" (Standards), "the structure says where everything lives" (Structure), "settings hold what can be turned on or off" (Settings), "agents decide from three tiers of memory" (Memory), "each part runs on the right model" (Intelligent model routing), "Teams can build their own harness on it." (Build your own harness). Each Proof line and the Must not has a feature: "The checks hold every part to the standard." (Check every part against the standard, fn-740); "Every tool reads its settings." (Make every tool read its settings); "Nothing an agent decides from sits outside memory." (Keep what agents decide from inside memory); the Must not (Check that no play, skill or sub-agent names a tracker).
+
+## 2. The domain: the six stay in software delivery planning
+- Added to the domain page's first paragraph, before "It does not release the work or run the product.": It also keeps the foundation every part stands on: the written standards and their checks, the builders a team uses for its own plays and loops, where everything lives, the settings, three tiers of memory, and the model each part runs on.
+- Also weighed: a domain of its own for the harness. It lost for the reason BI-7's, BI-8's and BI-9's did: a new domain page now would be redone at the parked name review, and no knowledge shelf covers it.
+
+## 3. The six capability pages that left the domain to the close
+On each: "It sits in software delivery planning with the other capabilities of Standards, structure, settings and memory. The domain's first paragraph does not name them yet; widening it is owed at this intent's close." becomes "It sits in software delivery planning with the other capabilities of Standards, structure, settings and memory, and the domain's first paragraph says so."; and one Sources line quoting the domain's new sentence is added after the last Sources line.
+
+## 4. A decision for the product owner: where a person says yes to moving memory up
+- Answer 6, as taken from the blanket yes, put that yes in Argus's Memory section, and the Memory pages say so.
+- The approved Argus page (`review-agent-work/fn-review-memory/feature.md` lines 30-31) says "STM - always STM" and "Moving memory up is not done here", from the review tool's design (`.garura/project/issues/617/specs/review-tool.md` lines 316-328), where the product owner's own words hold every update in short-term memory, and moving memory up is two plays of its own: "`/learn-memory` (short-term to Garura memory) and `/promote-memory` (Garura to Phoenix memory, approved outside Garura) are their own plays — planned, not filed yet."
+- Recommended: keep the design. The Memory pages drop "in the Memory section of Argus" and name the two planned plays as where the person says yes; Argus stays as approved. The Memory lines to change are shown before they are applied.
+- The other way: follow answer 6. Argus's two lines change to take the yes there, and the Memory page's open point is closed.
