+# feat: the product model rules say what ADR 043 changed — who writes each kind, loose nodes, Sources
+
+Kind: Story — a slice of the product model ontology (#597).
+
+What: the product model rules (the ontology, the spine and the page templates) say what ADR 043 changed.
+1. "No play owns a kind" (ontology, ADR 031 section 2) and "inside Kickoff each play writes one kind" are reconciled in the ontology's own text, not only in its log.
+2. The Functionality kind and the functionality template say a functionality may wait in the holding folder `functionalities/` with no capability until it is linked.
+3. The template headers name the play that writes each page today: /vision writes domains; /understand writes capabilities and functionalities.
+4. "Sources" is used for one thing only. The ontology already has a kind called Source (evidence kept under `_evidence/intent/`); the new `## Sources` section on pages (links, screen quotes, shelf references) needs its own name, or the ontology defines both.
+
+Why: the review of PR #691 (#689) found the rules changed in the spine and the log, but the ontology's kind text and the templates still say the old thing. A reader cannot tell which rule holds. Example: the capability template says /intent or /vision writes it; only /understand does now.
+
+Done when:
+- [ ] the ontology text, the spine and every template agree on who writes each kind and where a loose node waits;
+- [ ] the ontology defines each meaning of "Sources" once;
+- [ ] the ontology check passes.
+
