+
+## Context
+- Read: Gather it from the memory layer. Look first at the project's existing memory and its existing implementation, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory. If none of them answers, research it and raise a memory update for a person to approve. For this part, look for: for the test writer, the test writer's part of each gathered story and the design's classes, functions and interfaces; for the builder, the builder's part, each story's collected context and the design; for both, the project's code, the way its tests are written and its test tool, its coding standards, the tests it already has, the dev sandbox where the code runs while it is built, the surface the slice names for its usage guide, and the ontology's rules for what a builder reads.
+- Never read: for the builder, the tests for the work, the test writer's part, the plan's evals folder, the eval, the slice's hidden file and any ICE page; for the test writer, the builder's code while it is written, the plan's evals folder, the eval, the slice's hidden file and any ICE page.
+- Persona: the developer, a person or an AI agent who builds the code, in two parts never held by the same one for the same work, the test writer and the builder; the technical lead confirms each departure from the design.
+- Scope: In: writing all the tests for the work and showing each one fails, running them apart from the builder, building all the code until every test passes, tidying it while they stay passing, writing the usage guide, and updating the tests and the code as the work changes. Out: gathering the work, the design and its updates, the evals and the hidden file, running the evals and the verdict, checking the code against the standards, fixing a defect, choosing and tracking the work and its statuses, landing the change, the dev sandbox, and the product model.
+
+## Expectation
+- Scenario: The technical lead of a clinic's booking product names the epic of a slice on a browser page, with two stories. The test writer writes seven tests from the two stories' expectations and the booking service the design names, and the check shows all seven fail because the service is not built. The builder, handed the design and its own part and no test, builds the service and the booking page; the check tells it one expected result still fails, the builder finishes it, and the check finds all seven and the project's earlier tests passing. The builder joins two repeated functions into one, the check finds every test still passing and no test file changed, and the builder writes the slice's usage guide. The build session's record shows no read of a test or the evals folder.
+- Done means: Someone other than the builder compares the check's records with the tests for the work, and finds every test failed before any code, passed after the build and after the tidy, no test file different between the first run and the last, and every test the project already had still passing.
+- Done means: Someone other than the builder reads what the builder was handed and the build session's record, and finds no test, test writer's part, eval, hidden file or evals folder in either.
