+- Scope: In: the fixed code, changed to the cause and the fix design until every test passes on the check's record; the check's runs at its three moments, through a tool on the model or service the setting names, with their records and fingerprints; telling the builder what still fails; the tidy; and the changes outside the fix design and the results that could not be made to pass, sent back. Out: the cause and the fix design and their updates, the tests, the evals, checking the code against the standards, landing the change, the dev sandbox, and keeping the setting.
+
+## Expectation
+- Scenario: In a bank's payments service, a bug says a transfer of exactly the daily limit is refused. Its cause: the limit check lets a transfer through only when it is below the limit, where a transfer up to the limit is meant, and the fix design names that one function. The project's test model setting sends the tests to a different model from the one that fixes the code, and a tool runs them. The builder is handed the cause and the fix design, and no test. It changes the comparison, asks the check, and the check's record shows all three tests for the fix and the project's earlier tests passing. The refusal message read the old rule, so the builder changes the message file too and writes why, and the reason goes to the technical lead. It then joins two lines that read the limit the same way, and the check's record shows every test still passing and every test file the same as at the first run. The fix session's record shows no read of a test file or the evals folder.
