+
+## Context
+- Read: Gather it from the memory layer. Look first at the project's existing memory and its existing implementation, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory. If none of them answers, research it and raise a memory update for a person to approve. For this part, look for: the product profile's accessibility level and its pass bar with how it is checked, each capability's accessibility need in the product model, the declared surfaces with who opens each and each one's accessibility guideline, how each surface is reached in the testing environment, the testing environment's declaration with its data and test accounts, which tests the deployment design places where, who the product owner named to walk the build, the records of the review of each change and of the build's separate test run, the decisions that moved the accessibility level, the earlier accessibility verdicts, and the project's setting for rounds.
+- Never read: the value of any secret other than the testing environment's own, which a test takes at run time and never writes down; anything in production, such as its data or its traffic; and any ICE page.
+- Persona: the product owner, who set the accessibility level, names who walks the build, reads the verdict and decides each finding a person must decide; the developer gets back, as a handoff plan, each finding the builder can fix alone.
+- Scope: In: testing the running build in the testing environment with tools and by a person's walk, the verdict against the profile's accessibility level and each surface's accessibility guideline, the handoff plans, and testing again as the product changes. Out: the review of each change with its accessibility rules, the tests inside the code, a story's own accessibility lines, the surface's other properties and guidelines and the screen design, the other kinds of testing, setting or moving the accessibility level, declaring surfaces, the deployment design and the environments, fixing, recording defects and running the plan.
+
+## Expectation
+- Scenario: A clinic's booking page is built, and its profile sets accessibility at high with a pass bar that names a standard and says a tool and a person's walk check it; the browser surface's accessibility guideline asks for the same and for every step to work with a keyboard alone. In the testing environment the tools find each field labelled and each text clear against its background. The person the product owner named walks the booking end to end: with a keyboard alone, the dialog that confirms the visit cannot be left; with a screen reader, each free slot is read out only as a button with no time. The product owner sees a verdict with those two parts failed, each with how it was checked. The developer gets two handoff plans: a patient using a keyboard alone cannot leave the confirmation dialog, so the keyboard must be able to close it; and a patient using a screen reader cannot tell the free slots apart, so each must say its time. No test, tool or rule is named. After the fix, the tools run and the person walks again, and both pass.
+- Done means: Someone other than the one who ran the tests and the one who walked runs the tools again and walks the same steps on the same built work, gets the same verdict, and finds every part of the pass bar, each capability's accessibility need and each surface's accessibility guideline with a pass, a fail or a reason it was not tested, each walk naming who walked and how, no part a person must check counted as passed by a tool, no test outside the testing environment, and each finding sent where its way back says.
