+### Removed lines
+
+- From "Benefit hypothesis": - Serves the product owner who has just installed Garura in a project, and every play that later reads the project's memory, runs its review or writes to its tracker. Today a project gets all of the memory and every review runs every rule, so a project with no database, no front end or no permissions still gets those standards and those findings, and a play can stop the first time the tracker lacks an issue type it files.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - Words this page uses. Phoenix memory is the one shared memory on a machine (the domain shelves, the standards, the templates) that every project on that machine draws from. A project's Garura memory is the project's own memory, read before Phoenix memory, which initialize writes for the first time; it is built from only the part of Phoenix memory the project needs and is never a copy of a Phoenix file. A memory block is the relevant part of Phoenix memory that one setup answer selects, down to the part of a file: for example only the checkout part of the commerce knowledge for a project that needs checkout and not the rest of commerce, the Java standards for a Java answer, or the React front-end standards for a React answer; a project gets a block only when its answers call for it.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - More words this page uses. The product profile is the project's record of its setup answers, kept in the project's product model, where every later play reads the facts about the project. A rule group is a set of the review's rules that check one kind of thing (for example front end, back-end service, database, running service, permissions or personal data); the review runs or skips a group as a whole. A stand-in is a type the tracker already has that the plays file a work item as when the tracker cannot make the type they need.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - Out: laying Phoenix memory on the machine and bringing it up to date. The Phoenix install owns it: it runs once per machine and lays Phoenix memory from the Phoenix memory repository. Initialize only reads Phoenix memory, through the memory tool, and never writes it.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - Out: the Garura install, which runs once per project and lays only the pieces (the plays, agents, skills, tools and the memory tool) and no memory. Initialize starts where that install stops, so a project has no Garura memory of its own until initialize runs, and the two never write the same files.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - Out: running a review and deciding its findings (the review play, /check-change, owns it). Initialize only records which rule groups fit; the review reads that record and names what it skipped.
+- From "Boundary (In / Out / Never)": - Out: shaping, planning and filing work items (/shape-slice and /shape-plan own them). Initialize makes sure the tracker can hold their types; it files no work item itself. The change to the filing plays (/shape-plan and the plays that file work items) that makes them read the type mapping initialize records and stop with a message that points to initialize when a type is missing and not mapped is a later story of this business intent, not filed yet.
+- From "Guiding rules": - When the memory tool cannot reach Phoenix memory, initialize stops and builds nothing, and says the Phoenix install must run on this machine. An empty answer there would look like "nothing fits" and leave the project with too little memory.
