+# Feature: Update the setup as the project changes
+
+## What it does
+When the project changes, the product owner runs Initialize again: each question proposes the earlier answer, the product owner confirms what still holds and corrects what changed, and the project's memory, its rule-group record and its tracker set-up follow the new answers, through the same features as the first run, with nothing else changed.
+- Words this page uses. The product profile is the record of the product's shape and the quality levels it commits to, kept in the project's product model; it is where a new run finds the earlier answers. A memory block is the relevant part of Phoenix memory that one answer selects, down to the part of a file (for example the database standards for a database answer, or only the checkout part of the commerce knowledge); the project's memory is built from it and is never a copy of a Phoenix file. A rule group is a set of the review's rules that check one kind of thing (for example database or front end), which the review runs or skips as a whole. The mapping is the record that tells the plays which of the tracker's own types to use for each issue type Garura files; a stand-in is a type the tracker already has, mapped in place of one it cannot make. The brief is the message Initialize shows the product owner, where they confirm, correct, or type yes or no.
+- Lets the product owner run initialize again at any time, when the project changes: it gains a database, a web surface, personal data or a new tracker, or it drops one. The set-up then follows the project without a reinstall.
+- It also runs when a new version of Garura asks something new. Upgrade a project hands that run here: "running the set-up again when a new version asks something new".
+- A second run reads the earlier answers from the product profile and proposes each one again with the earlier answer as its source, so the product owner confirms what still holds and corrects only what changed.
+- From the new answers it rebuilds the project's memory, the review rule-group record and the tracker set-up, through the same features as the first run.
+- The same run also takes in, after the product owner's yes, each part of a block that changed in Phoenix memory since the block was built (Update each project's own memory as Phoenix memory changes, fn-883). It rebuilds the relevant part from Phoenix memory; it never copies the new file.
+- Examples, in different kinds of project:
+  - A school's timetable app gains a database: the database block comes into its memory, and the database rule group is kept.
+  - A bank's payments service moves its work from GitHub to Jira: the issue types are set up in Jira after the yes, and nothing changes in GitHub.
+  - A team's command-line tool takes a new Garura version that adds a question: the brief marks that question as new and proposes every other earlier answer.
+- How it meets the capability: the capability's last check, "The product owner can run initialize again when the project or Phoenix memory changes, and the set-up follows the change", is met here.
+
+Status: not built, like the rest of Initialize.
+
+## Inputs / Outputs
+- In: the earlier answers, as the product profile records them.
+- In: the project as it is now, for proposals where the project has changed.
+- In: the questions as they are now, which a change to Phoenix memory or a new version of Garura can change.
+- In: the product owner's typed reply to the brief: a confirmation or a correction for each question, and a yes or no for each tracker change.
+- Out: the questions, each with the earlier answer proposed and named as its source.
+- Out: the rebuilt memory, rule-group record and tracker set-up, through Build the project's memory (fn-project-memory), Choose the review rule groups (fn-review-rule-groups) and Set up the tracker's work-item types (fn-881).
+- Out: the change record, a list of what changed since the last run, shown in the brief: answers changed, memory blocks and parts added or taken out, rule groups now kept or skipped, tracker types made or mapped, the tracker the mapping now points to, and the old mapping when it changed.
+
+## Rules & behavior
+- An earlier answer is a proposal, never an answer, on a new run. The product owner confirms it again, because the project may have changed. The reason: #862's rule, "The answers are the person's; a proposed answer always names its source."
+- When the project itself now shows a different answer than the earlier one (for example a database it did not have), the proposal names the project as its source and the earlier answer is shown beside it, because what the project holds now is newer evidence than an old answer, and the earlier answer beside it lets the product owner see what changed before confirming.
+- A run with every answer unchanged and no change in Phoenix memory changes nothing, so a repeat run is always safe to start. The reason: the capability's reliability need, "a second run with every answer unchanged and no Phoenix memory change changes 0 files in the project's memory, its rule-group record and its tracker".
+- The tracker rule holds on every run: nothing is made in the tracker and no stand-in is mapped before a typed yes, because the tracker is an outside system the whole team sees, and a re-run is as able to make a wrong type as a first run.
+- When a changed answer rules out something the first run built (for example the database answer goes from yes to no), the re-run takes the database block out of the project's memory, marks the review's database rule group as skipped with the new answer, and records both in the change record, because a standard or a rule group the project no longer needs gives only noise.
+- When the tracker answer changes (for example from GitHub to Jira), the re-run sets up the issue types Garura files in the new tracker as a first run does, and makes nothing and maps nothing before the product owner's typed yes. After that yes the mapping points to the new tracker, and the change record keeps the old mapping. The old tracker stays as it is, with no type made, changed or removed there, because it may still hold the team's work items.
+- When the product owner says no to the tracker changes, the memory and rule-group changes from the other confirmed answers still apply, the mapping still points to the old tracker, and the change record shows the tracker part as declined, so the next run proposes the tracker set-up again. A yes to the answers is not a yes to a write in an outside system.
+- A run never removes an issue type from the tracker, because a type may hold work items the team still uses.
+- When the product profile holds no earlier answers (the first run never finished), the run asks the questions as a first run does and says in plain words that no earlier answers were found, so the product owner knows nothing is carried over.
+- When a question has no earlier answer while the others do (a question new since the last run, because Phoenix memory changed or a new version of Garura asks something new), the run asks it as a first run does, with a proposal and its source named, and marks it as new in the brief, so the product owner sees it is not a carried-over answer.
+- Not decided: where the change record is kept.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+- Given a project that was initialized before, when the product owner runs initialize again, then each question proposes the earlier answer from the product profile and names it as its source.
+- Given the product owner changes the database answer from no to yes, when the run ends, then the project's memory holds the database block and the review's database rule group is kept.
+- Given the database answer changes from yes to no, when the run ends, then the database block is no longer in the project's memory and the database rule group is recorded as skipped.
+- Given the tracker answer changes from GitHub to Jira and the product owner types yes to Jira's missing types, when the run ends, then those types are made in Jira, the mapping points to Jira, the change record keeps the old GitHub mapping, and nothing in the GitHub tracker changes.
+- Given the tracker answer changes, the database answer changes from no to yes, and the product owner says no to the tracker changes, when the run ends, then nothing is made in the new tracker, the mapping is unchanged, the project's memory holds the database block, the database rule group is kept, and the change record shows the tracker part as declined.
+- Given a project initialized before and a question added since that run, when initialize runs again, then the brief marks that question as new and proposes an answer with its source named, and every other question proposes its earlier answer.
+- Given a product profile with no earlier answers, when initialize runs again, then it asks every question as a first run does and says that no earlier answers were found.
+- Given every answer confirmed unchanged and no change in Phoenix memory, when the run ends, then no memory file, rule-group record or tracker type changes.
+- Given an issue type no longer needed, when initialize runs again, then the type stays in the tracker.
+
+## Out of scope
+- The first run's questions, memory, rule groups and tracker types: Ask the setup questions (fn-setup-questions), Build the project's memory (fn-project-memory), Choose the review rule groups (fn-review-rule-groups) and Set up the tracker's work-item types (fn-881) own them; this feature reuses them.
+- Finding and taking in a change to Phoenix memory under the blocks the project already holds: Update each project's own memory as Phoenix memory changes (fn-883) owns it; this feature only runs it as part of the same run.
+- Moving the old tracker's work items to the new tracker: no part of initialize does it, because initialize files and moves no work item.
+- Installing Garura again or moving it to a new version: Install into a project and Upgrade a project own them, and neither writes the project's memory.
+
+## Sources
