+## Intent
+- Goal: Orchestration keeps one list of the guardrails, and each entry names the shortcut it guards, the play that owns that work and its way past; when a guardrail meets its shortcut, it names the play to run and the way past where the person sees them, and each pass made on purpose writes one owed entry, so the guardrails the install lays keep agents to the plays without ever stopping a person.
+- Limit: Every guardrail has a way past, and each pass made on purpose is recorded.
+- Limit: The way past is named where the person sees it, and is never hidden.
+- Limit: Laying the guardrails, their form in each coding tool, the run marker and the review's check stay with their owners.
+- Fails if: A guardrail refuses a person's action with no way past named.
+- Fails if: An entry of the list leaves out what it guards, the play that owns the work, or its way past.
+- Fails if: A person passed a guardrail on purpose and no owed entry names it.
+
+## Context
+- Read: Gather it from the memory layer. Look first at the project's existing memory and its existing implementation, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory. If none of them answers, research it and raise a memory update for a person to approve. For this part, look for: the shortcuts #893 names and what it proposes, the guard on the evals folder, the play that owns each piece of work, what the coding tool can do before an agent acts and how it shows a reason, and the install's record of what it lays.
+- Never read: any memory file by its path, and any ICE page.
+- Persona: the developer, who works with agents in a project and sometimes means to take a shortcut on purpose.
+- Scope: In: the list of guardrails, what each guards, the play that owns its work, its way past, and the owed entry on each pass. Out: laying the guardrails, their form in each coding tool, the run marker in each play, the review's check, and where what is owed is kept.
+
+## Expectation
+- Scenario: In a shop's checkout project with the guardrails laid, an agent runs a play's bundled script by hand with no run of that play on record. The guardrail names the play to run and its way past, and the developer sees both. The developer means to go on, uses the way past, and the script runs; the issue's folder gains one owed entry naming the guardrail, who passed it and when. In a team's harness repository, an agent's edit to a compiled play is met the same way, and the guardrail names /edit-play.
+- Done means: Someone other than the one who built it reads the list and finds each entry naming what it guards, the play that owns the work and its way past; takes each listed shortcut in a project with the guardrails laid and finds the play and the way past named where the person sees them; and passes one on purpose and finds its owed entry.
