+## Context
+- Read: Gather it from the memory layer. Look first at the project's existing memory and its existing implementation, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory. If none of them answers, research it and raise a memory update for a person to approve. For this part, look for: each play's checkpoints and stops with their declared kind; the gate rule; the ways past that are built and what each records; the approved pages that name a way past or a stop; and #992, with its ADR once it is written.
+- Never read: any memory file by its path, and any ICE page.
+- Persona: the developer or the product owner, who meets a gate while running a play and wants to see how to go on.
+- Scope: In: the declaration of each gate's kind, way past and record; showing the way past at the gate; the owed entry on each use; and the list of today's gates with none. Out: the play checker's rules, adding a gate to a play, the gate settings, the ADR itself, where what is owed is kept, and the pinned yeses.
+
+## Expectation
+- Scenario: In a bank's payments service, a play needs a tracker type that is missing and not mapped. The gate's declaration names its kind, its way past and what using the way past records; the developer sees the way past at the gate, uses it, and the work goes on, and the issue's folder holds one owed entry naming the missing type, who went on and when. In the same week, the list of today's gates for #992's ADR names a plan session's stop on uncommitted work, with what its way past would record or why it keeps a person's yes.
+- Done means: Someone other than the one who built it reads every gate today's plays declare and finds each naming its kind, its way past and what using it records, or on the list with why it keeps a person's yes; uses one way past and finds its owed entry; and finds no gate of the stock-take missing from the list.
